WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.583 --> 00:00:02.335
- Welcome to the "Next 25."

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:04.212
I'm your host, Dwyane Wade.

00:00:04.212 --> 00:00:06.631
And today I get an opportunity to talk

00:00:06.631 --> 00:00:10.468
about the future for some of the game's brightest and best.

00:00:10.468 --> 00:00:12.804
We wanna talk about what we call "pipeline."

00:00:12.804 --> 00:00:14.264
But we're in this space right now

00:00:14.264 --> 00:00:18.685
where you have individuals becoming famous

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:20.562
before they have a driver's license.

00:00:20.562 --> 00:00:21.438
- Shady Baby.

00:00:21.438 --> 00:00:26.276
- [Chuckling] My daughter.
- [Laughing]
- She ain't even spoke too many words,

00:00:26.276 --> 00:00:27.861
and she was already famous, right?

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:29.029
What do you guys think about that

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:31.865
from the standpoint of the world that we live in in sports,

00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:34.659
that a young kid in high school can become famous?

00:00:34.659 --> 00:00:35.493
How do you deal with that?

00:00:35.493 --> 00:00:36.745
How do you handle that at a young age?

00:00:36.745 --> 00:00:41.332
- I feel like nowadays social media is the top of the top.

00:00:41.332 --> 00:00:44.878
Like, everybody goes to social media to look at things.

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:49.883
And guys now, like, are dunking at 13, 14 years old.
- Nasty.

00:00:50.008 --> 00:00:53.511
- You pull a dunk out in the game at 13, 14, they post it

00:00:53.511 --> 00:00:55.055
and you go viral.

00:00:55.055 --> 00:00:58.558
- They be having millions of the followers, 500K.

00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:01.186
All these people commenting on their life.

00:01:01.186 --> 00:01:04.522
They gotta live up to expectations at such a young age.

00:01:04.522 --> 00:01:06.149
They come out with rankings with kids

00:01:06.149 --> 00:01:09.277
that class of 2027, 2028.
- [Chuckling]

00:01:09.277 --> 00:01:10.570
- They're, like, how old is that?
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:11.780
- How old is that?

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.406
- It's crazy.

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:15.784
- You don't know what this kid can be.

00:01:15.784 --> 00:01:19.204
- Instagram famous is real.

00:01:19.204 --> 00:01:20.205
♪♪♪

00:01:20.205 --> 00:01:21.790
My name is Chris Johnson,

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:24.375
better known as NBA master trainer.

00:01:24.375 --> 00:01:27.962
I'm located here in Los Angeles, California.

00:01:27.962 --> 00:01:30.465
Just because you're Instagram famous doesn't mean

00:01:30.465 --> 00:01:33.927
that you're prepared to play in the WNBA or NBA.

00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:37.722
If you really go look at the track record of people

00:01:37.722 --> 00:01:41.309
that have had success from a social media period

00:01:41.309 --> 00:01:44.521
and continue that success, it's very, very slim.

00:01:44.521 --> 00:01:46.856
I think social media's gonna be around.

00:01:46.856 --> 00:01:48.608
It's not going anywhere.

00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:52.779
So it's really important that we provide the necessary tools

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:55.990
for young players coming up to understand

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:59.244
that social media is their new resume.

00:01:59.244 --> 00:02:01.246
- Is it good for our game, though?
- I don't…

00:02:01.246 --> 00:02:02.831
- Or is it bad for our game?

00:02:02.831 --> 00:02:04.582
- I think it's bad.
- Yeah.

00:02:04.582 --> 00:02:06.251
- I think it goes back to the highlight thing.

00:02:06.251 --> 00:02:08.002
Like, you don't actually know if someone's good.

00:02:08.002 --> 00:02:10.380
Because if you put together a highlight reel, like,

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.257
it's obviously gonna be the best stuff.
- Right.
- Right.
- Right.

00:02:12.257 --> 00:02:14.008
- And then, you know what I mean?

00:02:14.008 --> 00:02:16.136
And then they have, especially the really young ones,

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:17.428
they have all this pressure.

00:02:17.428 --> 00:02:19.222
And to have, like, millions of people disappointed

00:02:19.222 --> 00:02:20.932
in you because you're not as good
- Right, yeah.

00:02:20.932 --> 00:02:24.644
- at 13 or younger, that's hard.

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:25.478
- I agree with you.

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:26.771
I feel like it's a bad thing.

00:02:26.771 --> 00:02:29.023
- As somebody who was, like, not highly recruited,

00:02:29.023 --> 00:02:31.609
when you see that,
- Mmhmm.
- I feel like that motivates you.
- OK.

00:02:31.609 --> 00:02:32.861
- You know what I'm saying?

00:02:33.987 --> 00:02:36.239
Like, when I see, you know, kids my age

00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:38.491
or younger than me getting, like, all this love,

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:40.326
then that makes me wanna go harder.

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:41.161
You know what I'm saying?
- Right, yeah.

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:43.371
- That makes me, like, I wanna be right there.

00:02:43.371 --> 00:02:44.205
You know what I'm saying?

00:02:44.205 --> 00:02:47.083
So for me, I think there's a good and a bad to it.

00:02:47.083 --> 00:02:51.129
But the era that we grew up in, like, Ballislife,

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:53.506
Hoopmixtape,
- Yeah, yeah.
- all that stuff, coming out with mixtapes,

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:55.341
kids are they 14?
- Yeah.
- You be like,

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:57.635
"Man, I gotta get in the gym."

00:02:57.635 --> 00:03:00.180
- When I played, I didn't get to see anybody.
- Yeah, right.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.556
- Unless I see them on the—

00:03:01.556 --> 00:03:02.807
- Yeah, unless you seen them in person.

00:03:02.807 --> 00:03:03.641
- Right?

00:03:03.641 --> 00:03:04.726
And so the game has evolved now

00:03:04.726 --> 00:03:07.145
that you can see a 14-year-old, right, doing stuff.

00:03:07.145 --> 00:03:08.313
And you like, "I gotta get on that level."
- Yeah.

00:03:08.313 --> 00:03:09.606
- I never seen that.

00:03:09.606 --> 00:03:10.440
You know, I didn't know who was the...

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:12.233
I heard some name, Darius Miles.

00:03:12.233 --> 00:03:13.860
I'm like, "Who is Darius Miles from East St. Louis?
- [Chuckling]

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.236
- "I don't know who that is."
- Right.

00:03:15.236 --> 00:03:17.405
- I never seen him until I got a chance to play

00:03:17.405 --> 00:03:18.364
against him or play with him.

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:21.659
Right now, you can go on to see, you can see an eight-year-old right now

00:03:21.659 --> 00:03:23.244
that's gonna play in the league
- Right.
- in a couple years.

00:03:23.244 --> 00:03:24.329
And you go, "OK."

00:03:24.329 --> 00:03:25.163
- Yeah, exactly.

00:03:25.163 --> 00:03:25.997
- I know his scouting report.

00:03:25.997 --> 00:03:26.831
- My eighth-grade year,

00:03:26.831 --> 00:03:30.043
that's when, like, that's when I first got on social media.

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:33.046
Cameras came to my games and started recording me play.

00:03:33.046 --> 00:03:36.216
And then I ended up getting an invite to, like, USA.

00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:37.926
And that's when my journey, I guess,

00:03:37.926 --> 00:03:39.886
began to kick off after that.

00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:41.554
- When I came out, it was like,

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:43.223
you go to high school for four years.

00:03:43.223 --> 00:03:44.599
You go to college for four years.

00:03:44.599 --> 00:03:46.476
And then hopefully you get drafted, right?

00:03:46.476 --> 00:03:48.853
And that is what someone would have told me

00:03:48.853 --> 00:03:51.189
if I said, "How do I get to the NBA?"
- Mmm.

00:03:51.189 --> 00:03:52.732
- That's the path.

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:54.943
If a kid come up to you guys today and say,

00:03:54.943 --> 00:03:57.612
"What is the easiest, the most secure path to get

00:03:57.612 --> 00:03:59.572
to the NBA or to get to the W?"

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:00.573
What do you tell 'em?

00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:02.075
- Tell 'em to go to college.

00:04:02.075 --> 00:04:02.909
- You tell 'em to go to college?

00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:05.578
- I feel like college gives you that platform

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:07.247
where you can just really succeed.

00:04:07.247 --> 00:04:08.957
I always been that college route.

00:04:08.957 --> 00:04:11.876
Like, when they told me about the G League, I said,

00:04:11.876 --> 00:04:14.254
"Nah, I ain't doing that.  You can just throw that away."
- Right.

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:15.546
- I said, "I'm going to college."

00:04:15.546 --> 00:04:18.716
They changed the whole trajectory of what I wanted to do.

00:04:18.716 --> 00:04:19.634
They said they wanted to put me

00:04:19.634 --> 00:04:21.761
at point guard, different things like that.

00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:23.721
Me just playing defense, being in that system,

00:04:23.721 --> 00:04:26.724
being long, athletic, being able to show what I can do.

00:04:26.724 --> 00:04:30.561
- I think college, the traditional route,

00:04:30.561 --> 00:04:33.314
will still always be the dominant,

00:04:33.314 --> 00:04:38.236
maybe the safest route because of development.

00:04:38.236 --> 00:04:41.823
It takes so long for the true brain to develop.

00:04:41.823 --> 00:04:46.828
Some people are, they grow later. They develop later.

00:04:47.912 --> 00:04:49.455
Like, their body changes.

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:51.916
And believe it or not,

00:04:51.916 --> 00:04:54.127
if you look at a lot of successful people,

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:57.630
it's not how fast they can get there. It's the journey.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:00.508
- I wasn't a highly recruited kid growing up.

00:05:00.508 --> 00:05:02.593
Iowa State was my first high major offer,

00:05:02.593 --> 00:05:04.721
and I knew that's where I was going right away.

00:05:04.721 --> 00:05:06.764
All my friends and family thought I was gonna redshirt.

00:05:06.764 --> 00:05:09.142
I ended up starting every game

00:05:09.142 --> 00:05:10.518
besides the first game of the season.

00:05:10.518 --> 00:05:11.352
- Wow.

00:05:11.352 --> 00:05:12.895
- I got to go to the USA with these guys

00:05:12.895 --> 00:05:14.355
before my sophomore year.

00:05:14.355 --> 00:05:17.358
And that's when I was really like, "I think I'm a pro."

00:05:17.358 --> 00:05:18.443
- I ended up going to prep school.

00:05:18.443 --> 00:05:22.196
Once I went to prep school at Mount Zion in Baltimore,

00:05:22.196 --> 00:05:23.114
everything changed.

00:05:23.114 --> 00:05:24.741
I didn't have no offers before that.

00:05:24.741 --> 00:05:28.494
Dayton ended up coming to watch me play

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:30.246
and gave me a scholarship right then and there.

00:05:30.246 --> 00:05:31.956
And I was, I went on a visit there

00:05:31.956 --> 00:05:34.250
and knew right away, like, I belonged there.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:39.005
But I redshirted my first year, played my second year,

00:05:39.005 --> 00:05:41.632
and then I went to a Nike Skills Academy camp.

00:05:41.632 --> 00:05:43.843
Once I went to that camp, I was like, OK,

00:05:43.843 --> 00:05:46.971
now I feel like I got a chance to make it to the next level.

00:05:46.971 --> 00:05:48.514
- I was a little more highly recruited.

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:50.058
I got my first offer in eighth grade

00:05:50.058 --> 00:05:51.976
to Mizzou
- Yeaah.
- from Missouri.

00:05:51.976 --> 00:05:53.561
And I wanted to, like, sign there right away.

00:05:53.561 --> 00:05:55.521
I'm like, "I got my first offer."

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:57.815
And I really, like, credit my parents. They did a great job.

00:05:57.815 --> 00:06:00.068
My dad's like, "We're gonna take all our visits."

00:06:00.068 --> 00:06:00.902
Blah, blah, blah.

00:06:00.902 --> 00:06:03.363
And when UConn recruited me, it was super exciting

00:06:03.363 --> 00:06:04.655
cuz, you know, they're awesome.
- Mmhmm.

00:06:04.655 --> 00:06:06.574
- But I originally didn't wanna go there

00:06:06.574 --> 00:06:07.575
because they're so big.

00:06:07.575 --> 00:06:10.203
I'm like: "I wanna beat UConn. Everyone goes there."
- Yeah.

00:06:10.203 --> 00:06:12.830
- And then I took my visit there, and they convinced me.

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:13.748
[Wade and Collier laughing]

00:06:13.748 --> 00:06:15.917
It was just, like, at that point I knew I wanted to play

00:06:15.917 --> 00:06:18.127
in the W. And they have so many players there,

00:06:18.127 --> 00:06:19.170
and they do so well.

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:21.422
I knew, I kind of treated it like a business thing.

00:06:21.422 --> 00:06:23.341
Like, I knew going there was gonna get me

00:06:23.341 --> 00:06:24.967
to the next step that I wanna be at.

00:06:24.967 --> 00:06:26.803
- College outside of education,

00:06:26.803 --> 00:06:27.762
because some of y'all just go in

00:06:27.762 --> 00:06:28.971
for a year and y'all outta there.
- Right.
- Yeah.

00:06:28.971 --> 00:06:31.224
- Y'all ain't get no education.
- [Laughing]

00:06:31.224 --> 00:06:34.727
- But I feel like you're learning how to be an adult.
- Yeah.

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:36.312
- You're learning how to be on your own.

00:06:36.312 --> 00:06:38.147
You're learning how to wash clothes.

00:06:38.147 --> 00:06:42.110
You're learning, like, all the things that you may, some kids may not have done

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:43.694
by being at home with their parents.

00:06:43.694 --> 00:06:45.696
Now you're learning it's all on you.

00:06:45.696 --> 00:06:47.907
And so before you get to the NBA level

00:06:47.907 --> 00:06:49.909
or the professional level,

00:06:49.909 --> 00:06:52.995
you have learned some things about how to be an adult.

00:06:52.995 --> 00:06:55.081
- Honestly, I feel like college is the last time

00:06:55.081 --> 00:06:56.833
that you're on a true team because

00:06:56.833 --> 00:06:59.794
when you get to the league, like, you get people traded midseason.

00:06:59.794 --> 00:07:02.588
Like, you don't know if you're gonna be there from year to year.
- [All expressing agreement]

00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:04.966
- That's the last time you feel, like, so a part of something,

00:07:04.966 --> 00:07:06.884
where you feel like you're so in the trenches
- [Murmuring agreement]

00:07:06.884 --> 00:07:08.302
- with that group of people.

00:07:08.302 --> 00:07:09.762
- And when you get to that March Madness,

00:07:09.762 --> 00:07:11.597
I think the boost in the March Madness…
- Yeah, that March Madness.

00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:13.766
- Ain't nothing like that March Madness.
- March Madness.
- Yeah.

00:07:13.766 --> 00:07:15.893
- Ain't nothing like that college experience.

00:07:15.893 --> 00:07:16.727
You know what I'm saying?

00:07:16.727 --> 00:07:17.812
- You got to have the college experience.
- It's not the same.

00:07:17.812 --> 00:07:20.731
- Yes, like, you're talking about adulting and all that stuff.

00:07:20.731 --> 00:07:21.566
Yes, that is a—

00:07:21.566 --> 00:07:23.359
- I am shocked that y'all said, everyone's like,

00:07:23.359 --> 00:07:24.610
college, college, college.

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:29.115
- Twenty-five years from now, we'll be still seeing players go

00:07:29.115 --> 00:07:30.992
to college where they're one-and-done,

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.954
two years and done or play all four.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:38.666
They will still have the same opportunity

00:07:38.666 --> 00:07:41.461
as the players that go an untraditional route.

00:07:41.461 --> 00:07:43.754
And the untraditional routes

00:07:43.754 --> 00:07:46.674
to me are in still, like, a beta phase.

00:07:46.674 --> 00:07:51.262
It's still unknown too what the failure rate is.

00:07:51.262 --> 00:07:55.975
Both routes could meet the end route,

00:07:55.975 --> 00:08:00.688
but I'm still a traditional route-runner because I believe

00:08:00.688 --> 00:08:04.734
that young people need an opportunity to be young.

00:08:04.734 --> 00:08:08.571
And some people can go the untraditional route

00:08:08.571 --> 00:08:10.948
maybe because of circumstances.

00:08:10.948 --> 00:08:13.618
- Now you have G League, right?

00:08:13.618 --> 00:08:17.163
Obviously, you have overseas. You have the Ignite team.

00:08:17.997 --> 00:08:20.750
You can be like Scottie and go to seven high schools.

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:21.876
- [All chuckling]

00:08:21.876 --> 00:08:23.294
- We've given all these opportunities

00:08:23.294 --> 00:08:24.795
for our basketball players to be able

00:08:24.795 --> 00:08:27.381
to, you know, have an opportunity to reach their dreams. It's more

00:08:27.381 --> 00:08:30.009
than just going to college for four years.

00:08:30.009 --> 00:08:30.885
How does this evolve?

00:08:30.885 --> 00:08:32.094
Where do we go from here?

00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:36.057
- I feel like resources now is a lot different
- Yeah, it's crazy.

00:08:36.057 --> 00:08:37.141
- than it used to be.

00:08:37.141 --> 00:08:39.810
I feel like there's a lot more people in this world

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:41.729
that have the in on things.

00:08:41.729 --> 00:08:43.356
There's mentors that you can go to

00:08:43.356 --> 00:08:46.192
that know people that know people.

00:08:46.192 --> 00:08:48.277
And it's, like, I feel like nowadays

00:08:48.277 --> 00:08:50.905
it's just a matter of who you know.

00:08:50.905 --> 00:08:53.950
Like, you know the right person, you gon' be good.

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:56.160
And if you have talent, you gon' be real good.

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.788
- With the Ignite program,

00:08:58.788 --> 00:09:00.998
you're gonna have to be probably one

00:09:00.998 --> 00:09:05.044
of the top three, four, five players in your class.

00:09:05.044 --> 00:09:09.549
So it makes it exceptional.

00:09:09.549 --> 00:09:14.095
And it is geared around about 10 players.

00:09:14.095 --> 00:09:17.348
And that system is truly built

00:09:17.348 --> 00:09:21.894
for player development and mental development.

00:09:21.894 --> 00:09:26.148
I think there could be a few more pathways.

00:09:26.148 --> 00:09:27.567
I don't know when the CBA is up,

00:09:27.567 --> 00:09:31.320
but being able to go straight to the league again, you know,

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:34.115
I would like to see that changed also.

00:09:34.115 --> 00:09:36.659
And just so it can switch it up.

00:09:36.659 --> 00:09:39.662
But that does put the curve in everything.

00:09:39.662 --> 00:09:41.038
- Once they change that college rule,

00:09:41.038 --> 00:09:44.917
and we get money in NIL deals,
- Man.
- it's way different now.

00:09:44.917 --> 00:09:46.961
- I'll be watching my brother get all these deals

00:09:46.961 --> 00:09:48.713
at Kentucky. I'm like,

00:09:48.713 --> 00:09:50.548
"Can I get something when I was in college?"

00:09:50.548 --> 00:09:52.174
- I'm rationing my per-diem check.

00:09:52.174 --> 00:09:53.926
- Shoutout Jacob Toppin.
- [Chuckling]

00:09:53.926 --> 00:09:54.927
- So is that where we're going,

00:09:54.927 --> 00:09:59.348
where the NBA is not the all-end, be-all?
- [Chuckling]
- Or the W, right?

00:09:59.348 --> 00:10:02.101
It's like kids growing up, it's, like, the W is still young,

00:10:02.101 --> 00:10:03.603
so it is still growing, right?
- Yeah.

00:10:03.603 --> 00:10:05.354
- The NBA is kind of older, so it's kinda, like,

00:10:05.354 --> 00:10:06.188
"Oh, I can make money.

00:10:06.188 --> 00:10:08.274
"I don't need to go to the league
- Right, yeah, yeah.
- to make money."

00:10:08.274 --> 00:10:10.067
"I can make money in high school."

00:10:10.067 --> 00:10:12.361
- I think that's where I was going before the NIL deal.

00:10:12.361 --> 00:10:14.363
Like, I heard a lot of people talking about playing overseas

00:10:14.363 --> 00:10:15.406
instead of going to college.
- Mmhmm.

00:10:15.406 --> 00:10:18.826
- And then we can't go into the draft until we're, like, 22.

00:10:18.826 --> 00:10:20.995
But now with the deals, you can make money there.

00:10:20.995 --> 00:10:23.122
I don't know if it's gonna change.

00:10:23.122 --> 00:10:27.376
- With the NIL, with your ability to be paid off

00:10:27.376 --> 00:10:30.463
of your own personal brand and likeliness,

00:10:30.463 --> 00:10:34.675
you're gonna see a lot of people not make it to the NBA

00:10:34.675 --> 00:10:37.803
because they're being paid a lot earlier.

00:10:37.803 --> 00:10:40.931
And that can actually take away

00:10:40.931 --> 00:10:44.727
from the hunger depending on the individual.

00:10:44.727 --> 00:10:45.936
I don't think making it

00:10:45.936 --> 00:10:50.775
to the NBA is the end be-all.  It's something that you want.

00:10:50.775 --> 00:10:52.735
I work with the best players in the world

00:10:52.735 --> 00:10:54.862
and some people that make an extreme amount

00:10:54.862 --> 00:10:57.406
of money playing basketball.

00:10:57.406 --> 00:11:01.911
They still get up…5 o'clock a.m.

00:11:01.911 --> 00:11:05.706
They still get up and do it over and over again.

00:11:05.706 --> 00:11:06.999
You have a better chance

00:11:06.999 --> 00:11:10.836
at doing other things than making it in the NBA.

00:11:10.836 --> 00:11:15.800
And that's how special those guys are to that craft.

00:11:16.217 --> 00:11:21.222
And that's how special they are to being on that stage

00:11:22.306 --> 00:11:23.808
and being able to perform.

